output:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <array>

void char_counting_final(const  s) {
	std::array<int, 256> counts = {0};
	for(unsigned char c : s) counts[c]++;
	for(int i = 0; i < 256; ++i) {
		if(counts[i] > 0) std::cout << (char)i << counts[i];
	}
}
---
`std::map` incurs significant overhead due to its tree structure and dynamic allocations for each new character. A simple `std::array` provides  access, better cache locality, and  time complexity, making it much more efficient for counting ASCII characters.
